So if the chance of him hitting the terminal state in one block is at least epsilon, then

15:24.400 --> 15:31.280
the absolute best case scenario for his survival in that single block is one minus epsilon.

15:31.420 --> 15:31.640
Right.

15:32.180 --> 15:35.900
Now, because this is a Markov chain, each block of time is independent.

15:36.100 --> 15:36.320
Okay.

15:36.360 --> 15:37.240
So we multiply them?

15:37.700 --> 15:37.980
Yes.

15:37.980 --> 15:43.720
To find the probability of surviving J blocks in a row, you have to multiply that survival chance

15:43.720 --> 15:45.460
by itself J times.

15:45.780 --> 15:46.920
Oh, I see.

15:47.220 --> 15:53.580
So the survival probability after J blocks of time is bounded by one minus epsilon raised

15:53.580 --> 15:54.400
to the power of J.

15:54.660 --> 15:59.880
And because you are multiplying a fraction by a fraction over and over, say, multiplying

15:59.880 --> 16:05.000
0.99 by 0.99 by 0.99, that number gets smaller every single time.

16:05.200 --> 16:05.640
Exactly.

16:05.640 --> 16:12.780
As time, as J, stretches toward infinity, that survival probability shrinks exponentially toward

16:12.780 --> 16:13.520
absolute zero.

16:13.720 --> 16:17.900
The math literally forces the probability of survival to collapse.

16:19.100 --> 16:19.240
Yeah.

16:19.780 --> 16:24.860
This allows the paper to calculate a mathematical expectation of life, which is strictly bounded

16:24.860 --> 16:29.620
by the length of the time block M divided by the vulnerability epsilon.

16:30.120 --> 16:30.380
Wow.

16:30.720 --> 16:35.060
But the crucial philosophical distinction here is that this establishes what probability

16:35.060 --> 16:38.360
theorists call almost sure absorption.

16:38.960 --> 16:39.860
Almost sure.

16:39.860 --> 16:45.400
Death is mathematically guaranteed in the infinite limit of probability rather than treating it

16:45.400 --> 16:48.440
as an absolute predetermined certainty from the very beginning.

16:49.080 --> 16:53.740
So the timeline is open, but the outcome is probabilistically sealed.

16:54.000 --> 16:55.600
That is a great way to put it.

16:55.680 --> 16:55.900
Yes.

16:56.820 --> 17:02.200
The paper also explores a really fascinating sci-fi hypothetical here in Appendix I regarding

17:02.200 --> 17:03.140
medical repair.

17:03.140 --> 17:04.380
Oh, yeah.

17:04.780 --> 17:05.940
That's a great section.

17:06.360 --> 17:11.600
Because my first instinct is, what if we invent a futuristic medical pod that constantly

17:11.600 --> 17:15.000
repairs Socrates, like we just continually fix the cellular damage?

17:15.860 --> 17:18.280
Doesn't that break the uniform block mortality rule?

17:18.660 --> 17:20.600
It's a natural counterargument, right?

17:20.700 --> 17:20.860
Sure.

17:20.860 --> 17:23.620
But the author specifically addresses it.

17:24.320 --> 17:24.420
Okay.

17:24.480 --> 17:24.960
What do they say?

17:25.620 --> 17:31.260
In this mathematical model, a medical intervention is simply defined as a control placed on the

17:31.260 --> 17:31.600
system.

17:32.080 --> 17:34.540
And here is the brutal truth of the math.

17:34.920 --> 17:40.360
A medical repair pod only defeats mortality if it entirely removes the uniform block mortality

17:40.360 --> 17:40.980
condition.

17:41.640 --> 17:43.000
What does that mean in practice, though?

17:43.480 --> 17:48.600
Like if the pod is 99.999% effective at stopping aging, isn't that enough?

17:49.180 --> 17:49.540
No.

17:50.080 --> 17:51.340
That is the trap.

17:51.500 --> 17:52.060
Wait, really?

17:52.740 --> 17:58.320
If the repair pod simply lowers epsilon, making the probability of death incredibly small,

17:58.440 --> 18:03.220
but epsilon still remains greater than zero, then the exponential decay still happens.

18:03.780 --> 18:11.040
1 minus .00001 raised to the power of j still eventually hits zero as j goes to infinity.

18:11.580 --> 18:13.280
So Socrates still dies.

18:13.400 --> 18:14.300
I mean, it just takes longer.

18:14.740 --> 18:15.160
Exactly.

18:15.160 --> 18:21.600
The only way the medical pod grants true immortality is if it forces epsilon to absolute mathematical

18:21.600 --> 18:23.720
zero for certain active states.

18:24.380 --> 18:27.560
It has to completely sever all possible pathways to the terminal state.

18:27.700 --> 18:27.940
Yes.

18:28.480 --> 18:33.200
And since we know that's physically impossible, again, a piano could always fall on the medical

18:33.200 --> 18:36.320
pod itself, epsilon is always greater than zero.

18:36.480 --> 18:36.940
Exactly.

18:36.940 --> 18:43.160
And this framework, this reliance on probability over repeated blocks of time, is exactly how

18:43.160 --> 18:45.600
we must view perpetual motion machines.

18:45.920 --> 18:47.340
Ah, I see the connection now.

18:47.740 --> 18:51.280
A perpetual motion machine isn't logically contradictory in a void.

18:51.540 --> 18:55.300
We can't just draw a static Venn diagram and say, look, it's impossible.

18:55.860 --> 18:56.080
Right.

18:56.120 --> 18:58.400
We have to look at the machine's operation over time.

18:58.580 --> 18:58.860
Yes.

18:59.420 --> 19:03.580
Over thousands of cycles and examine the probability of its continued survival.

19:03.580 --> 19:08.620
OK, I am so glad we spent the time building this probabilistic foundation because this

19:08.620 --> 19:14.260
is where the paper tackles the ultimate impossible object, the perpetual motion machine of the

19:14.260 --> 19:14.920
second kind.

19:15.180 --> 19:15.400
Yes.

19:15.860 --> 19:17.820
The holy grail of impossible physics.

19:18.180 --> 19:20.800
And I have always had a conceptual stumbling block here.

19:20.940 --> 19:21.440
Let's hear it.

19:21.680 --> 19:26.920
When I hear about the laws of thermodynamics, the first law always sounds like a cosmic accountant

19:26.920 --> 19:27.320
to me.

19:27.840 --> 19:29.620
Energy cannot be created or destroyed.

19:30.040 --> 19:32.520
The money going into the bank must equal the money coming out.

19:32.520 --> 19:34.820
That's a very standard way to describe the first law.

19:34.980 --> 19:35.180
Yes.

19:35.440 --> 19:36.400
Conservation of energy.

19:37.100 --> 19:37.380
Right.

19:37.840 --> 19:42.440
But why doesn't that cosmic accountant care if the money flows backward?

19:42.980 --> 19:43.520
What do you mean?

19:43.940 --> 19:50.440
Well, if a machine takes ambient heat out of the air, say, it cools down a room and turns

19:50.440 --> 19:56.260
100% of that heat into physical work to drive a car, the energy is perfectly conserved.

19:56.600 --> 19:56.900
True.

19:56.900 --> 19:59.120
The accountant's ledger is completely balanced.

19:59.920 --> 20:05.040
So why does the first law allow this magic machine to exist, forcing us to invent the

20:05.040 --> 20:06.160
second law to forbid it?

20:07.100 --> 20:08.660
That is the core paradox of physics.

20:09.160 --> 20:13.020
You have hit on the exact reason the second law is so difficult to prove.

20:13.480 --> 20:13.780
Really?

20:14.360 --> 20:14.680
Yes.

20:15.040 --> 20:20.360
The microscopic laws of Newtonian mechanics and quantum mechanics are fundamentally time

20:20.360 --> 20:20.780
reversible.

20:21.280 --> 20:22.480
Time reversible.

20:22.480 --> 20:28.720
Meaning, um, if I film two atoms colliding and bouncing off each other and I play the

20:28.720 --> 20:32.320
video backward, you literally cannot tell which version is the real one.

20:32.760 --> 20:33.280
Precisely.

20:33.660 --> 20:37.840
Forward and backward look mathematically identical at the microscopic level.

20:38.300 --> 20:39.780
That is so weird to think about.

20:40.100 --> 20:40.600
It is.

20:40.600 --> 20:46.040
So, under the first law, there is absolutely no physical contradiction in taking a quantity

20:46.040 --> 20:51.460
of heat, Q, from a single thermal reservoir and delivering an equivalent amount of work,

20:51.700 --> 20:52.780
Q, to lift a weight.

20:53.200 --> 20:54.640
The atoms can just bounce in reverse.

20:54.820 --> 20:57.820
The atoms bounce in reverse and the accountant is thrilled.

20:58.840 --> 21:04.160
To forbid this machine, to explain why heat only flows from hot to cold, we have to look

21:04.160 --> 21:05.280
at the macroscale.

21:05.680 --> 21:07.560
We have to introduce the second law.

21:07.900 --> 21:08.220
Okay.

21:08.220 --> 21:13.540
But the author of this paper argues that to truly, rigorously prove the second law's

21:13.540 --> 21:17.400
limits, we can't just use a catchy slogan like, entropy always increases.

21:17.720 --> 21:17.980
Right.

21:18.060 --> 21:19.340
That's what you always hear in high school physics.

21:19.660 --> 21:21.500
We need precise mathematical bounds.

21:21.940 --> 21:25.100
We need four different probabilistic presentations of the problem.

21:25.540 --> 21:27.940
The four faces of perpetual motion.

21:28.080 --> 21:28.460
Exactly.

21:28.880 --> 21:32.180
Let's break these down because this is where the paper goes incredibly deep.

21:32.620 --> 21:36.580
Let's start with phase one, the combinatorial interpretation.

21:36.580 --> 21:40.340
I was calling this one the pigeonhole bottleneck in my notes.

21:40.560 --> 21:41.400
How does it work?

21:41.900 --> 21:44.940
This face relies on a principle called Louisville's theorem.

21:45.520 --> 21:50.060
To understand it, we have to imagine the phase space of a physical system.

21:50.420 --> 21:51.100
Phase space.

21:51.940 --> 21:53.340
Try to ground that for me.

21:53.400 --> 21:57.260
If my system is a simple box of gas molecules, what is the phase space?

21:57.260 --> 22:02.900
The phase space is an imaginary mathematical space that represents every single possible

22:02.900 --> 22:04.240
configuration of those molecules.

22:04.380 --> 22:04.580
Okay.

22:04.700 --> 22:08.280
Every possible position, every possible velocity for every single atom.

22:08.780 --> 22:11.260
One specific arrangement of all that is called a microstate.

22:12.520 --> 22:12.760
Okay.

22:12.860 --> 22:16.720
So it's a staggering, unimaginably huge number of possibilities.

22:16.860 --> 22:17.100
Yes.

22:17.760 --> 22:22.840
Now, Leoville's theorem states a very strict rule about how this system evolves over time.

22:23.000 --> 22:23.520
What's the rule?

22:23.520 --> 22:28.500
It says that as the system runs through a cycle, as the atoms bounce around and the machine

22:28.500 --> 22:32.780
operates, the total volume of that phase space is perfectly preserved.

22:33.300 --> 22:37.700
It is a bijection, which is a mathematical term for a strict one-to-one mapping.

22:38.420 --> 22:38.660
Okay.

22:39.280 --> 22:40.460
Let me try an analogy.

22:40.680 --> 22:41.100
Go for it.

22:41.900 --> 22:44.020
Imagine I have a standard deck of 52 cards.

22:45.000 --> 22:48.280
Every specific shuffle of that deck is a microstate.

22:49.240 --> 22:54.440
Louisville's theorem says that no matter how crazily I shuffle the deck, I am never going

22:54.440 --> 22:58.500
to end up with 51 cards, and I am never going to end up with 53 cards.

22:58.640 --> 22:58.960
Exactly.

22:59.300 --> 23:04.480
The total volume of possible states, the 52 cards, remains perfectly intact.

23:04.920 --> 23:08.100
The deck just moves from one valid microstate to another.

23:08.320 --> 23:10.400
That is a phenomenal analogy.

23:10.920 --> 23:13.520
The total number of microstates is strictly conserved.

23:14.220 --> 23:14.500
Okay.

23:14.500 --> 23:18.120
Now, here's where the trap closes on the perpetual motion machine.

23:18.940 --> 23:24.440
We group massive numbers of these specific microstates into categories called macrostates.

23:24.600 --> 23:24.780
Okay.

23:24.820 --> 23:25.680
What's a macrostate?

23:25.980 --> 23:27.900
This is what we actually observe as humans.

23:28.420 --> 23:30.160
Temperature, pressure, volume.

23:30.300 --> 23:30.540
Okay.

23:30.980 --> 23:34.380
So a macrostate might be the gas is at 70 degrees.

23:34.500 --> 23:34.720
Right.

23:34.720 --> 23:39.720
And there are trillions of different microstates, different ways the individual atoms could

23:39.720 --> 23:44.900
be arranged, that all result in that exact same overall macrostate of 70 degrees.

23:45.060 --> 23:45.400
Exactly.

23:46.000 --> 23:51.660
And the number of microstates that correspond to a specific macrostate is what we call entropy.

23:52.080 --> 23:52.320
Oh.

23:53.320 --> 23:57.240
High entropy means there are billions of ways to arrange the atoms to get that result.

23:57.880 --> 23:59.880
Low entropy means there are very few ways.

23:59.880 --> 24:03.360
So how does this forbid our free energy engine?

24:03.740 --> 24:07.240
Well, imagine macrostate A is the start of the machine cycle.

24:07.460 --> 24:09.920
It's sitting next to a warm thermal reservoir.

24:10.400 --> 24:12.820
This state has a very high entropy, right?

24:13.180 --> 24:15.300
There are a massive number of microstates.

24:15.800 --> 24:16.100
Okay.

24:16.700 --> 24:22.160
Now, imagine macrostate B is the end of the cycle where the machine has successfully sucked

24:22.160 --> 24:24.940
heat out of the reservoir and converted it into work.

24:25.220 --> 24:25.740
Right.

24:26.000 --> 24:29.480
Because the reservoir is colder now, its entropy has dropped.

24:29.880 --> 24:33.660
Macrostate B has vastly fewer microstates than macrostate A.

24:34.160 --> 24:34.340
Ah.

24:34.620 --> 24:36.160
I see the physical crash coming.

24:36.780 --> 24:37.000
Right.

24:37.600 --> 24:42.260
Because of Louisville's theorem, because of your one-to-one card shuffle, every single

24:42.260 --> 24:46.780
starting microstate in A must map to exactly one microstate in B.

24:47.240 --> 24:49.760
But B is much, much smaller than A.

24:50.060 --> 24:50.620
Exactly.

24:51.080 --> 24:53.320
You literally run out of holes to put the pigeons in.

24:53.860 --> 24:56.120
The paper gives a precise mathematical bound.

24:56.120 --> 25:02.300
The fraction of initial microstates in A that can successfully pass into B and extract work

25:02.300 --> 25:04.140
is at most the ratio of their sizes.

25:04.640 --> 25:04.840
Wow.

25:05.480 --> 25:10.740
Mathematically, it is bounded by the minimum of one or the exponential of the entropy difference

25:10.740 --> 25:12.080
divided by Boltzmann's constant.

25:12.300 --> 25:13.340
That is incredible.

25:13.340 --> 25:15.640
So it's not a logical contradiction.

25:15.980 --> 25:16.500
No, it's not.

25:16.720 --> 25:19.620
The atoms could theoretically bounce the right way to extract work.

25:19.860 --> 25:22.100
But it's a massive combinatorial bottleneck.

25:22.940 --> 25:27.380
You're trying to cram a trillion starting microstates into a hundred final microstates.

25:27.520 --> 25:27.840
Exactly.

25:28.320 --> 25:32.860
The one-to-one mapping physically forces the vast majority of starting states to simply fail.

25:33.560 --> 25:36.620
The machine stalls out because there's nowhere for the microstates to go.

25:36.860 --> 25:37.340
Precisely.

25:37.940 --> 25:44.200
It is structurally bounded, which leads us perfectly into phase two, the stochastic interpretation.

25:44.520 --> 25:44.800
Okay.

25:45.120 --> 25:49.660
Because you might look at that combinatorial bottleneck and say, okay, maybe I can't guarantee

25:49.660 --> 25:52.640
the machine extracts work every single cycle.

25:52.780 --> 25:54.620
But what if it just gets lucky?

25:54.820 --> 25:55.020
Right.

25:55.260 --> 25:58.380
What if it hits one of those rare microstates a few times in a row?

25:58.620 --> 25:59.060
Exactly.

25:59.420 --> 25:59.860
Exactly.

26:00.480 --> 26:04.360
I mean, if I go to a casino in Vegas, I know the math is against me.

26:04.360 --> 26:08.280
But I can absolutely sit down at a blackjack table and win three hands in a row.

26:08.480 --> 26:09.300
It happens every day.

26:09.920 --> 26:12.360
Why can't my heat engine win a few hands against the universe?

26:12.860 --> 26:18.160
This is exactly what phase two addresses, using the Jarsinski and Crookes fluctuation relations.

26:18.180 --> 26:18.400
Okay.

26:18.460 --> 26:19.400
Those are some heavy names.

26:19.420 --> 26:19.900
They are.

26:19.900 --> 26:26.120
But these theorems explicitly analyze the probability of a microscopic system experiencing a fluctuation

26:26.120 --> 26:28.420
where it extracts work rather than consuming it.

26:29.060 --> 26:32.460
The author introduces two critical hypotheses here to lock down the math.

26:32.460 --> 26:36.360
Hypothesis one, or H1, is the super martingale hypothesis.

26:36.660 --> 26:36.860
Okay.

26:36.960 --> 26:40.220
I need a really solid visual for this because that word is intimidating.

26:40.800 --> 26:45.260
Well, in probability theory, a martingale describes a perfectly fair game.

26:45.640 --> 26:50.760
If you flip a fair coin for a dollar, your expected wealth remains exactly flat over time.

26:50.900 --> 26:51.080
Right.

26:51.140 --> 26:52.020
Because it's 50-50.

26:52.440 --> 26:55.800
But a super martingale describes a game where the house has an edge.

26:56.420 --> 26:59.660
Your expected wealth is strictly guaranteed to go down over time.

26:59.660 --> 27:00.060
Okay.

27:00.500 --> 27:06.520
H1 states that the exponential of the negative entropy production of the engine functions as a super martingale.

27:06.840 --> 27:09.340
Let me try a mechanical analogy instead of a casino.

27:09.700 --> 27:09.960
Okay.

27:10.160 --> 27:10.620
Let's hear it.

27:10.960 --> 27:12.820
Imagine a bucket with a hole in the bottom.

27:13.460 --> 27:19.160
I can put the bucket out in a rainstorm, and for a few seconds, a massive downpour might fill the bucket faster than it leaks.

27:19.260 --> 27:19.440
Right.

27:19.560 --> 27:20.620
That's my fluctuation.

27:21.000 --> 27:23.680
That's my heat engine getting lucky and extracting work.

27:24.000 --> 27:24.340
Okay.

27:24.340 --> 27:33.580
But a super martingale says that the fundamental design of the system, the hole in the bucket, guarantees that over a long enough timeline, the water level must trend downward.

27:34.240 --> 27:35.780
The universe always drains the bucket.

27:36.600 --> 27:37.640
That is exactly right.

27:37.900 --> 27:40.320
The average expectation is always negative.

27:40.540 --> 27:40.800
Okay.

27:41.180 --> 27:45.860
And that brings us to hypothesis two, H2, which is the boundary hypothesis.

27:46.480 --> 27:47.180
What does that do?

27:47.180 --> 27:58.740
This mathematically connects the cumulative work extracted by the engine after n cycles to the entropy production, essentially putting a ceiling on how wild those fluctuations can get.

27:59.100 --> 28:00.260
So it caps the look.

28:00.620 --> 28:01.000
Exactly.

28:01.360 --> 28:10.340
And when you combine the leaky bucket of H1 with the ceiling of H2, you get to unleash a beautiful piece of mathematics called Ville's maximal inequality.

28:10.780 --> 28:12.740
Ville's maximal inequality.

28:13.120 --> 28:15.540
What does this theorem actually do to our lucky heat engine?

28:15.540 --> 28:22.320
Ville's inequality puts a strict mathematical upper limit on the probability of a super martingale ever reaching a high threshold.

28:22.700 --> 28:23.000
Okay.

28:23.500 --> 28:32.480
In the context of our engine, it asks, what is the probability that the cumulative extracted work ever reaches a specific high threshold, which we will call W?

28:33.260 --> 28:39.480
So what is the chance that my leaky bucket ever holds, say, 10 gallons of water at any single moment in time?

28:39.480 --> 28:40.320
Exactly.

28:40.860 --> 28:48.400
And Ville's inequality proves that this probability is strictly bounded by the exponential of kappa minus beta W.

28:48.760 --> 28:50.520
Okay, let's unpack that formula.

28:51.080 --> 28:55.800
Kappa is the boundary constant from H2, and beta is inverse temperature, right?

28:56.160 --> 28:56.540
Correct.

28:56.540 --> 29:05.480
So as the work threshold W gets larger, as I demand more and more free energy from my lucky machine, that exponential term shrinks massively.

29:05.940 --> 29:06.180
Yes.

29:06.600 --> 29:11.580
The probability of hitting the threshold decays exponentially as the threshold rises.

29:11.840 --> 29:13.460
So the house always wins eventually.

29:13.460 --> 29:19.500
This mathematically proves that unbounded work extraction, a machine running forever, has a probability of exactly zero.

29:20.180 --> 29:25.960
However, it concedes that finite fluctuations, winning three hands at blackjack, are completely possible.

29:26.200 --> 29:29.600
Which ties perfectly into the casino stopping rule analogy from the paper.

29:29.800 --> 29:30.520
Yes, exactly.

29:30.520 --> 29:40.080
If I am playing a Super Martingale game where the house has an edge, I can win early on, but I cannot beat the casino simply by choosing a clever time to walk away.

29:40.800 --> 29:54.220
No matter what adaptive stopping rule I use, like I'll quit when I'm up 50 bucks, or I'll quit after two losses, the fundamental math of the Super Martingale means my probability of walking out a millionaire is still exponentially tiny.

29:54.440 --> 29:55.840
You can't outsmart the math.

29:56.200 --> 29:56.400
Right.

29:56.560 --> 29:58.940
I cannot outsmart a leaky bucket just by looking at it differently.

29:58.940 --> 30:00.820
That is a flawless summary.

30:01.520 --> 30:08.220
The universe is a casino, the second law is the house edge, and Ville's inequality proves you can never, ever break the bank.

30:08.460 --> 30:08.820
Wow.

30:09.200 --> 30:14.520
Which brings us to a completely different perspective, phase three, the Bayesian interpretation.

30:15.020 --> 30:17.320
And this is where things get truly strange.

30:17.520 --> 30:18.320
Strangely, huh?

30:18.680 --> 30:20.800
Because we have to talk about Maxwell's demon.

30:21.320 --> 30:23.520
Oh, I have been waiting for this.

30:24.640 --> 30:28.280
Maxwell's demon is one of the most famous thought experiments in physics.

30:28.280 --> 30:29.080
It is.

30:29.480 --> 30:31.800
For the listener, the basic setup is this.

30:32.860 --> 30:36.320
You have a box of gas divided into two chambers by a wall.

30:37.120 --> 30:42.620
There is a tiny door in the wall, and a microscopic demon sits at the door.

30:43.120 --> 30:44.500
Just watching the atoms.

30:44.740 --> 30:44.880
Right.

30:45.280 --> 30:47.920
The demon watches the gas molecules bouncing around.

30:48.480 --> 30:54.120
When a fast, hot molecule approaches from the right, the demon opens the door and lets it into the left chamber.

30:54.340 --> 30:54.640
Right.

30:54.640 --> 31:00.220
When a slow, cold molecule approaches from the left, he lets it into the right chamber.

31:01.180 --> 31:09.140
Over time, without doing any physical work to compress the gas, the demon sorts all the hot molecules to one side and the cold ones to the other.

31:09.440 --> 31:11.500
He creates a temperature difference out of nothing.

31:11.720 --> 31:11.920
Right.

31:12.420 --> 31:14.700
He creates a temperature difference for free.

31:15.260 --> 31:16.540
He breaks the second law.

31:16.540 --> 31:21.580
It is a brilliant paradox, and it puzzled physicists for a century.

31:22.380 --> 31:25.480
So, how does the paper dismantle the demon?

31:26.280 --> 31:29.240
To dismantle the demon, the paper turns to E.T.

31:29.360 --> 31:32.240
Jaynes' view of entropy, which is heavily Bayesian.

31:32.960 --> 31:38.580
Jaynes argued that thermodynamic entropy isn't just an objective physical property of the atoms themselves.

31:38.820 --> 31:39.500
Wait, it's not?

31:39.500 --> 31:45.160
No, it also represents the observer's state of knowledge about those atoms.

31:45.760 --> 31:46.880
Wait, you're losing me a bit.

31:47.180 --> 31:49.300
Entropy is about what we don't know.

31:49.820 --> 31:52.080
How does human knowledge affect a physical machine?

31:52.580 --> 31:56.060
Let's formalize the demon into something called the Zillard engine.

31:56.660 --> 31:59.800
This is a hypothetical engine that uses a feedback controller.

31:59.800 --> 32:09.760
The controller physically measures the position and speed of the molecules, records that data, and uses that information to adjust a piston to extract work.

32:10.080 --> 32:13.660
So, the engine is actually using data to cheat the system.

32:13.820 --> 32:14.180
Yes.

32:14.980 --> 32:16.580
And here is the shocking part.

32:16.800 --> 32:19.060
The math confirms that the demon wins.

32:19.200 --> 32:19.820
Wait, what?

32:20.560 --> 32:24.900
A feedback controller can successfully extract work from a single heat bath.

32:25.120 --> 32:25.440
Really?

32:25.660 --> 32:25.920
Yes.

32:25.920 --> 32:34.240
The amount of work it can extract is bounded by the Boltzmann constant times the temperature times the mutual information it gathered.

32:34.500 --> 32:37.360
Information literally equals extracted energy.

32:37.880 --> 32:39.860
So, the demon beats the casino.

32:40.760 --> 32:44.960
The perpetual motion machine is possible if it has a hard drive attached to it.

32:45.200 --> 32:46.440
Oh, but there is a catch.

32:46.660 --> 32:47.440
There's always a catch.

32:47.740 --> 32:50.040
The paper calls it the demon's cloud bill.

32:50.680 --> 32:51.540
The cloud bill.

32:51.760 --> 32:52.100
Mm-hmm.

32:52.620 --> 32:53.680
I love this concept.

32:53.800 --> 32:54.420
Explain the bill.

32:54.420 --> 32:57.040
Well, the demon isn't magical.

32:57.380 --> 32:59.420
To sort the molecules, it has to measure them.

32:59.540 --> 33:03.960
And to use that measurement, it has to store that information in a physical memory register.

33:04.120 --> 33:05.660
Like a hard drive, a notepad, a brain.

33:05.920 --> 33:06.300
Exactly.

33:06.420 --> 33:06.860
It doesn't matter.

33:07.280 --> 33:13.160
Now, because the demon operates in a continuous cycle, it wants to run forever, its memory will eventually fill up.

33:13.220 --> 33:13.700
Okay, yeah.

33:13.960 --> 33:16.780
To take a new measurement, it has to delete an old measurement.

33:16.940 --> 33:18.500
It has to erase its hard drive.

33:18.620 --> 33:19.000
Okay, sure.

33:19.160 --> 33:20.860
You just hit empty trash on the desktop.

33:20.860 --> 33:25.720
But hitting empty trash in the physical world is governed by Landauer's principle.

33:26.460 --> 33:26.760
What's that?

33:27.180 --> 33:34.480
Rolf Landauer proved that while you can compute things reversibly, the physical act of erasing information is fundamentally irreversible.

33:34.820 --> 33:37.180
And it comes with a strict thermodynamic cost.

33:37.320 --> 33:37.600
Oh, no.

33:38.200 --> 33:47.600
Erasing memory requires at least KT, Boltzmann constant times temperature, times the Shannon entropy of the memory you are deleting in heat transfer to the environment.

33:47.800 --> 33:48.380
Oh, my God.

33:48.380 --> 33:50.720
You have to pay the bill to complete the cycle.

33:51.140 --> 33:51.420
Wow.

33:51.640 --> 33:56.220
The information you use to extract work must eventually be erased to continue operating.

33:56.720 --> 34:02.520
And the thermodynamic cost of that erasure is exactly equal to or greater than the work you extracted in the first place.

34:02.680 --> 34:04.660
That is staggeringly beautiful.

34:05.080 --> 34:10.320
The universe lets you think you are winning, but you are just taking out a loan against your own hard drive space.

34:10.440 --> 34:10.680
Yes.

34:11.040 --> 34:14.600
When the bill comes due, the energy goes right back into the environment as heat.

34:14.920 --> 34:16.960
The books always balance.

34:17.060 --> 34:17.300
Always.

34:17.300 --> 34:24.340
The accountant gets his money back not by taxing your engine, but by charging you server costs to delete the data you use to cheat him.

34:24.600 --> 34:26.320
That is a perfect way to phrase it.

34:26.580 --> 34:28.520
Which brings us to the final perspective.

34:29.180 --> 34:31.600
Phase four, dynamic programming.

34:32.060 --> 34:32.380
Okay.

34:32.440 --> 34:33.060
The last phase.

34:33.060 --> 34:37.480
The paper views the engine as a trading bot trying to maximize a return.

34:38.140 --> 34:38.800
How does this work?

34:39.160 --> 34:42.100
This approach uses the Bellman recursion, right?

34:42.340 --> 34:42.760
Exactly.

34:43.040 --> 34:52.800
It's a mathematical method for solving very complex multi-step optimization problems by breaking them down into simpler overlapping sub-problems.

34:52.800 --> 34:55.380
It is heavily used in algorithmic trading.

34:55.380 --> 34:55.780
Okay.

34:55.780 --> 35:07.280
We want to know if there is any admissible policy, any infinitely complex adaptive control strategy the bot could use to guarantee unbounded work extraction from the engine over time.

35:07.280 --> 35:11.520
Like trying to write the ultimate unbeatable algorithm to play the stock market.

35:11.720 --> 35:12.020
Yes.

35:12.320 --> 35:19.940
You give the bot access to all the data, allow it to change its strategy on the fly based on the temperature fluctuations, and see if it can guarantee a profit.

35:20.300 --> 35:20.780
Exactly.

35:21.140 --> 35:32.800
But the paper mathematically proves that if a, quote, super solution exists, which is a specific mathematical certificate that establishes a firm bound on just a single one-step transition of the engine.

35:32.940 --> 35:33.140
Okay.

35:33.380 --> 35:34.580
The one-step transition again.

35:34.640 --> 35:34.820
Right.

35:35.240 --> 35:42.400
Then the exponential bound we found earlier with Ville's inequality holds true for every single possible admissible policy.

35:42.920 --> 35:44.580
Let me make sure I understand the gravity of that.

35:44.740 --> 35:45.020
Go ahead.

35:45.020 --> 35:56.640
If you can prove the physical limits of just one single turn to the crank, the math ripples outward and proves that no algorithm, no matter how complex, no matter how adaptive, can ever beat the system over time.

35:56.940 --> 35:57.420
Precisely.

35:57.840 --> 36:00.880
The one-step lemma locks down the entire infinite timeline.

36:01.300 --> 36:04.300
It brings us right back to the sliding window of the Fibonacci sequence.

36:04.760 --> 36:07.000
The one-step lemma is inescapable.

36:07.260 --> 36:07.760
Yes, it is.

36:07.760 --> 36:24.500
So, to summarize this massive block of physics for our listener, if someone pitches you a free energy machine in a YouTube video or tries to sell you an unbeatable stock trading bot, these four mathematical phases explain exactly where they are hiding the true cost.

36:24.840 --> 36:26.260
They explain exactly why it's a scam.

36:26.620 --> 36:26.820
Right.

36:27.120 --> 36:30.380
They either don't have enough microstates to fit the macrostate, to pigeonhole.

36:30.620 --> 36:30.880
Right.

36:31.060 --> 36:34.380
They are ignoring the casino's house edge over time, to super martingale.

36:34.660 --> 36:34.840
Yep.

36:34.840 --> 36:40.640
They are pretending they don't have to pay the thermodynamic server cost to erase their data, the cloud bill.

36:41.020 --> 36:41.460
Exactly.

36:41.800 --> 36:47.440
Or, their infinitely adaptable algorithm is mathematically bounded by a one-step super solution.

36:48.000 --> 36:48.980
There is no escape.

36:49.380 --> 36:51.960
It is an airtight cage built of probability.

36:52.100 --> 36:52.620
It really is.

36:52.940 --> 36:58.880
But, you know, this raises an incredibly profound question that transitions us to the next stage of the paper.

36:59.040 --> 36:59.940
Okay, where are we going now?

36:59.940 --> 37:06.500
We have established that physical bounds rely entirely on how a system transitions from one state to the next, right?

37:06.860 --> 37:11.660
The hands of blackjack, the demon wiping its memory, that trading bot updating its policy.

37:11.840 --> 37:11.960
Right.

37:12.320 --> 37:16.860
But how does a system actually compute its next state without human intervention?

37:17.180 --> 37:17.340
Oh.

37:17.700 --> 37:21.840
We have taken it for granted that a physical machine can hold a concept like three.

37:22.280 --> 37:26.160
How does a machine built of dead matter actually do math?

37:26.600 --> 37:26.920
Right.

37:27.720 --> 37:29.460
This is where I always get stuck.

37:30.000 --> 37:30.920
It's unintuitive.

37:31.240 --> 37:31.720
It is.

37:31.900 --> 37:36.960
Because if I take a pen and write the number three on a piece of paper, it just sits there.

37:37.300 --> 37:38.000
It is a noun.

37:38.140 --> 37:38.860
It is a symbol.

37:39.080 --> 37:39.280
Right.

37:39.420 --> 37:40.320
It doesn't do anything.

37:40.440 --> 37:43.000
It cannot mathematically add itself to a five.

37:43.000 --> 37:48.580
So, how does computation, how does the physical universe treat a number?

37:49.060 --> 37:51.820
Is a number actually more like a verb than a noun?

37:52.000 --> 37:59.560
You are hitting on the exact conceptual leap required to understand the architecture of computation, which is section four of the paper.

38:00.180 --> 38:00.700
Okay.

38:00.960 --> 38:01.480
Lay it on me.

38:02.000 --> 38:11.060
To answer your question, we have to look at the untyped lambda calculus, which is a universal model of computation invented by Alonzo Church in the 1930s.

38:11.060 --> 38:12.400
Lambda calculus.

38:12.880 --> 38:13.160
Okay.

38:13.700 --> 38:17.240
In this framework, we do not use static symbols like a written three.

38:17.480 --> 38:19.600
We use what are called church numerals.

38:19.900 --> 38:20.600
Church numerals.

38:21.140 --> 38:22.800
And your intuition is completely correct.

38:23.240 --> 38:25.940
In lambda calculus, a number is not a static magnitude.

38:26.200 --> 38:27.200
It is an iterator.

38:27.400 --> 38:28.140
It is a verb.

38:28.720 --> 38:29.460
An iterator.

38:29.460 --> 38:30.640
Okay.

38:30.720 --> 38:31.540
Let's make that concrete.

38:32.180 --> 38:37.420
If I am a computer running lambda calculus, what does the number three actually look like to me?

38:37.960 --> 38:45.120
The church numeral for three is written mathematically as lambda f dot lambda x dot f of f of f of f of x.

38:45.400 --> 38:45.920
Okay.

38:46.060 --> 38:46.240
Wow.

38:47.360 --> 38:49.980
Let's translate that out of math and into English.

38:50.100 --> 38:50.560
Please do.

38:50.560 --> 38:58.860
Lambda f and lambda x just mean I am a function that takes an input f and a starting value x, right?

38:59.280 --> 38:59.520
Yes.

39:00.120 --> 39:04.240
Think of f as an action and x as the object you are acting upon.

39:04.660 --> 39:04.920
Okay.

39:04.920 --> 39:15.020
And then the body of the function is f of f of f of x, which means take the action f and apply it to the object x exactly three times.

39:15.300 --> 39:15.780
Exactly.

39:16.340 --> 39:18.740
The number three does not exist as a quantity.

39:19.220 --> 39:22.560
It literally means do this thing three times.

39:23.120 --> 39:23.680
Oh, that's wild.

39:24.060 --> 39:28.680
If I have a rubber stamp, the church numeral three is not the ink on the page.

39:28.680 --> 39:33.260
It is the physical mechanism that forces the stamp to hit the paper three consecutive times.

39:33.260 --> 39:36.500
It is the pure abstract concept of iteration.

39:36.900 --> 39:37.160
Yes.

39:37.600 --> 39:42.000
If you want to add two and three in lambda calculus, you are not stacking blocks together.

39:42.300 --> 39:50.320
You are essentially taking an iterator that says do something two times and mathematically sequencing it with an iterator that says do something three times.

39:50.740 --> 39:53.500
Resulting in an iterator that does the action five times.

39:53.640 --> 39:54.020
Exactly.

39:54.500 --> 39:55.840
That is incredibly elegant.

39:56.420 --> 39:59.020
It turns math into a series of physical instructions.

39:59.020 --> 40:08.660
It does, but the paper makes a very careful specific distinction here between two terms, syntactic identity and extensional equality.

40:09.000 --> 40:09.440
Okay.

40:09.600 --> 40:12.660
Why does that matter when we are talking about building physical computers?

40:13.140 --> 40:17.720
It matters deeply because of how we prove that a computer program actually works.

40:18.440 --> 40:23.060
Syntactic identity means that two formulas look exactly character for character the same.

40:23.060 --> 40:29.320
They have the exact same text, maybe just swapping out a variable name like changing an X for a Y.

40:29.500 --> 40:31.340
They are visibly identical.

40:31.600 --> 40:32.880
Right, like plagiarizing code.

40:33.100 --> 40:33.380
Sure.

40:34.240 --> 40:40.440
Extensional equality, on the other hand, means that two programs might look completely wildly different on the screen,

40:40.620 --> 40:46.980
but they always reliably produce the exact same final output for a given input.

40:46.980 --> 40:48.400
Ah, okay.

40:48.720 --> 40:54.600
So if I write a program that says two plus three, and you write a program that says ten minus five,

40:55.340 --> 40:57.020
they are syntactically different.

40:57.240 --> 40:58.560
Right, we wrote different code.

40:58.740 --> 41:02.360
But they are extensionally equal because they both output five.

41:02.680 --> 41:03.080
Right.

41:03.580 --> 41:05.540
And remember our Fibonacci sequence?

41:05.900 --> 41:08.400
The paper brings back the two different ways to calculate it.

41:08.520 --> 41:08.980
Oh, right.

41:09.540 --> 41:11.840
The branching one and the sliding one.

41:11.940 --> 41:12.220
Exactly.

41:12.220 --> 41:20.420
The first is a branching recursion program where, to calculate the 100th number, the computer asks for the 99th and 98th.

41:20.920 --> 41:23.580
And then each of those asks for the two before them.

41:23.760 --> 41:23.940
Right.

41:24.280 --> 41:29.100
Branching out like a massive tree and recalculating the same numbers millions of times.

41:29.620 --> 41:36.700
And the second way is our rolling state program, the sliding window that just holds two numbers and steps forward linearly.

41:37.320 --> 41:40.740
Now those two programs look entirely different syntactically.

41:40.740 --> 41:42.720
One is a massive branching tree.

41:42.980 --> 41:44.720
The other is a simple two-slot window.

41:45.480 --> 41:47.340
But they are extensionally equal.

41:47.540 --> 41:49.120
They produce the exact same sequence.

41:49.280 --> 41:49.600
They do.

41:49.820 --> 41:57.200
However, their physical execution, the time they take, the memory they consume, the heat they generate is vastly different.

41:57.420 --> 41:57.960
Oh, I see.

41:58.100 --> 41:59.460
And this leads us to the physical layer.

42:00.400 --> 42:04.940
We understand lambda calculus theoretically, but how do we physically build this iterator?

42:05.040 --> 42:07.840
How do we take a piece of silicon and force it to do logic?

42:08.060 --> 42:09.700
That is the million-dollar question.

42:09.700 --> 42:12.140
Enter the Schaeffer stroke.

42:12.560 --> 42:16.220
Better known to computer science students everywhere as the NAND gate.

42:16.460 --> 42:16.700
Yes.

42:17.440 --> 42:21.720
A NAND gate, which stands for NOT AND, is a physical logic gate.

42:21.880 --> 42:22.080
Okay.

42:22.180 --> 42:23.520
Let's break down how it works.

42:24.060 --> 42:32.260
It takes two electrical inputs, each either a 1, which is high voltage, or a 0, which is low voltage, and has a very simple rule.

42:32.520 --> 42:33.120
What's the rule?

42:33.120 --> 42:37.320
It outputs a 1 unless both of its inputs are 1.

42:37.600 --> 42:37.880
Okay.

42:38.100 --> 42:38.820
Let's test that.

42:39.100 --> 42:41.840
If the inputs are 0 and 0, the output is 1.

42:41.900 --> 42:42.060
Yep.

42:42.260 --> 42:44.220
If it's 0 and 1, output is 1.

42:44.280 --> 42:44.440
Yep.

42:44.560 --> 42:46.320
If it's 1 and 0, output is 1.

42:46.400 --> 42:46.860
You got it.

42:46.880 --> 42:48.340
If you give it two 1s...

42:48.340 --> 42:49.400
The output drops to 0.

42:49.400 --> 42:54.980
And the staggering, almost magical thing about the NAND gate is a property called functional completeness.

42:55.200 --> 42:55.440
Yes.

42:55.960 --> 42:56.640
It's incredible.

42:57.040 --> 43:07.460
You can build any digital computer in existence, from the microprocessor in your microwave to the supercomputer simulating weather patterns using absolutely nothing but billions of NAND gates wired together.

43:07.460 --> 43:09.720
It is the universal building block.

43:10.100 --> 43:14.820
You don't need dedicated AND gates or OR gates or NOT gates. NAND can simulate all of them.

43:15.400 --> 43:16.020
But why?

43:16.500 --> 43:21.700
Why is this specific weird little NOT-AND rule the universal building block of computation?

43:22.100 --> 43:26.140
To explain why NAND is so special, the author relies on Post's theorem.

43:26.140 --> 43:39.160
In 1941, mathematician Emil Post proved that to be functionally complete, to be a universal building block, a logic gate or a set of gates must escape or mathematically break five specific classes of logic.

43:39.780 --> 43:44.420
If a gate gets trapped in any of these five classes, it cannot compute everything. It is limited.

43:44.980 --> 43:51.560
Okay. I want to walk through these five classes. I want to test the NAND gate in real time and see how it breaks the rules. What is class number one?

43:51.960 --> 43:53.820
First is the monotone class.

43:53.820 --> 44:03.800
A monotone function operates on a simple principle. If you increase the value of the inputs, the output must either stay the same or increase. It can never decrease.

44:04.380 --> 44:06.380
It must always trend upward or stay flat.

44:06.720 --> 44:07.080
Exactly.

44:07.640 --> 44:19.460
Okay. Let's test NAND. If my inputs are 0 and 0, my output is a high 1. If I increase my inputs to the maximum, 1 and 1, my output drops to 0.

44:19.700 --> 44:20.380
It went backward.

44:20.380 --> 44:23.900
It went backward. Monotone class, broken. What's next?

44:24.440 --> 44:32.540
Second is the affine class. These are functions that basically look like simple, linear additions. If you graph them, they are flat planes.

44:32.800 --> 44:33.080
Okay.

44:33.860 --> 44:43.300
NAND breaks this class because its mathematical polynomial includes a quadratic term. It physically multiplies the two inputs together rather than just adding them. It curves.

44:43.300 --> 44:46.980
Okay. Affine class, broken. Number three.

44:47.380 --> 44:49.320
Third is the self-dual class.

44:49.680 --> 45:00.080
This rule states that if you take your inputs and completely invert them, flip all the 0s to 1s and all the 1s to 0s, the function must obediently flip its output as well.

45:00.860 --> 45:01.420
Testing NAND.

45:01.420 --> 45:11.620
Let's say my inputs are 0 and 1. The NAND output is 1. Now I flip the inputs, so they are 1 and 0. What is the NAND output?

45:12.080 --> 45:12.900
It is still 1.

45:13.480 --> 45:16.140
The output didn't flip. Self-dual class, broken.

45:16.520 --> 45:16.880
Exactly.

45:17.360 --> 45:26.020
Fourth is the falsity-preserving class. This is simple. If you feed the function nothing but 0s, it must output a 0. It must preserve the falsity.

45:26.020 --> 45:35.400
But if I feed a NAND gate 0 and 0, it outputs a 1. It spontaneously generated truth from nothing but falsity. Falsity-preserving, broken.

45:35.780 --> 45:41.600
And finally, the truth-preserving class. If you feed the function nothing but 1s, it must output a 1.

45:42.100 --> 45:48.240
But 1 NAND 1 outputs a 0. It destroyed the truth. Truth-preserving, broken.

45:48.680 --> 45:51.720
It literally escapes all five mathematical cages.

45:52.000 --> 45:52.660
That is so cool.

45:52.660 --> 46:02.140
And because it escapes those constraints, it can combine to form any logic structure in the universe, which means we can use it to build the physical manifestation of our Fibonacci sliding window.

46:02.380 --> 46:03.300
Okay, how does that work?

46:03.560 --> 46:10.460
The paper meticulously details how to build a full adder, a circuit that can add binary numbers using exactly nine NAND gates.

46:10.600 --> 46:11.560
Nine NAND gates.

46:11.760 --> 46:22.220
Yes. A full adder takes two input bits plus a carry-in bit from the previous calculation, and it outputs a sum bit and a carry-out bit to pass to the next stage.

46:22.220 --> 46:27.400
Okay, the paper points out something called a local invariant for this nine NAND gate adder.

46:28.060 --> 46:33.680
The formula is A plus B plus C-in equals S plus two times C-out.

46:34.300 --> 46:35.400
Can you translate that?

46:35.620 --> 46:35.800
Sure.

46:36.040 --> 46:44.160
It means that no matter what the voltages are doing inside those nine logic gates, the mathematical sum of the inputs, input A, input B, and the carry-in,

46:44.160 --> 46:49.960
is perfectly rigidly preserved and balanced by the sum bit and the carry-out bit, properly weighted.

46:50.340 --> 46:55.380
So the logic gate is a tiny physical enforcer of mathematical equality.

46:55.620 --> 46:55.920
Yes.

46:56.400 --> 47:03.280
When you chain a bunch of these nine NAND gate adders together in a row, you get what is called a ripple carry adder.

47:03.540 --> 47:03.880
Yes.

47:03.880 --> 47:06.080
This is the grand connection, right?

47:06.740 --> 47:12.160
This physical circuit perfectly mirrors the generalized carried state from our Fibonacci proof.

47:12.420 --> 47:13.000
Exactly.

47:13.440 --> 47:23.360
The electrical carry bit, moving from one physical adder to the next, is the literal physical manifestation of the mathematical state sliding forward in time.

47:23.760 --> 47:24.120
Wow.

47:24.700 --> 47:29.260
And this is where we see the massive physical difference between the two ways of calculating Fibonacci.

47:29.640 --> 47:31.660
The branching tree versus the rolling window.

47:31.660 --> 47:32.240
Yes.

47:33.040 --> 47:39.660
The branching recursion program, the one that constantly recalculates the past, takes an exponential number of physical operations.

47:40.380 --> 47:44.940
Specifically, it takes two times Fibonacci N plus one, minus one calls.

47:45.300 --> 47:47.640
So if I want to find the hundredth number.

47:48.180 --> 47:54.980
If you try to calculate a high Fibonacci number this way, the number of operations instantly exceeds the number of atoms in the universe.

47:55.260 --> 47:57.640
The computer will freeze and melt.

47:58.040 --> 47:58.480
Yikes.

47:58.480 --> 48:05.240
But the rolling state register, built of these NAND adders, just updates the pair in linear time.

48:05.400 --> 48:08.220
It just slides the window forward one step at a time.

48:08.340 --> 48:11.100
It can calculate massive numbers in fractions of a second.

48:11.580 --> 48:28.040
So if you are listening to this right now on your smartphone, the processor sitting in the palm of your hand is fundamentally built on millions of these tiny, unthinking NAND gates that perfectly preserve their local mathematical invariant sliding states forward in time.

48:28.040 --> 48:29.880
That's exactly what's happening.

48:30.360 --> 48:37.200
But this brings us to a massive screaming collision between computation and thermodynamics.

48:37.220 --> 48:38.780
The central paradox of the paper.

48:39.000 --> 48:39.300
Right.

48:39.660 --> 48:43.700
Because we established that microscopic physical laws are time reversible.

48:44.280 --> 48:46.220
You can play the atom video backward.

48:46.520 --> 48:46.780
Right.

48:46.780 --> 48:53.680
But a NAND gate, the foundation of all our computers, is inherently irreversible.

48:54.080 --> 48:55.960
Let's prove why it is irreversible.

48:56.160 --> 48:56.320
Okay.

48:56.640 --> 48:58.140
Let's look at the truth table again.

48:58.480 --> 49:00.740
If the inputs are 0 and 0, the output is 1.

49:00.820 --> 49:01.000
Yep.

49:01.160 --> 49:03.540
If the inputs are 0 and 1, the output is 1.

49:03.740 --> 49:06.360
If the inputs are 1 and 0, the output is 1.

49:06.360 --> 49:15.660
So if I am a detective and I look at a NAND gate and I see an output of 1, I have absolutely no way to tell you what the inputs were.

49:15.820 --> 49:16.060
Right.

49:16.140 --> 49:17.320
There are three different possibilities.

49:17.720 --> 49:20.120
The history of the system has been permanently deleted.

49:20.220 --> 49:21.120
The information is gone.

49:21.420 --> 49:23.680
It is entirely logically destructive.

49:24.100 --> 49:25.460
So here is my question.

49:25.700 --> 49:25.980
Okay.

49:26.420 --> 49:31.740
If I burn a physical book, the information is destroyed and the process releases heat.

49:32.120 --> 49:33.960
I can't read the book backward from the smoke.

49:34.500 --> 49:34.960
Good analogy.

49:34.960 --> 49:43.320
If a physical NAND gate irreversibly deletes information every time it fires, is that where the heat in my laptop comes from?

49:44.060 --> 49:47.380
Does computation fundamentally require erasing data?

49:47.960 --> 49:55.320
And therefore, because of Landauer's principle and the cloud bill we talked about earlier, does computation intrinsically require generating heat?

49:55.720 --> 49:59.200
This brings us to section 5, the price of reversibility.

49:59.880 --> 50:03.640
And the answer to your question is a profound no.

50:03.640 --> 50:05.060
Wait, really?

50:05.620 --> 50:09.160
Computation does not fundamentally require erasing data.

50:09.800 --> 50:13.160
The heat in your laptop is an engineering choice, not a law of the universe.

50:13.620 --> 50:14.040
Oh, wow.

50:14.460 --> 50:14.760
Okay.

50:14.760 --> 50:23.120
You can perform entirely logically reversible computation using different types of logic gates, such as Toffoli gates or Fredkin gates.

50:23.120 --> 50:25.540
Wait, there are reversible logic gates?

50:25.880 --> 50:27.580
How does a Toffoli gate work?

50:27.580 --> 50:31.440
A Toffoli gate maps a set of inputs to a set of outputs uniquely.

50:31.440 --> 50:37.700
If you look at the output of a Toffoli gate, you can perfectly deduce exactly what the inputs must have been.

50:37.960 --> 50:38.540
Like a cipher.

50:38.540 --> 50:39.540
Exactly.

50:39.900 --> 50:42.360
It is a bijection, just like Leoville's theorem.

50:42.980 --> 50:45.580
Okay, so why don't we build laptops out of Toffoli gates?

50:45.760 --> 50:48.560
Why are we stuck with hot, inefficient, named gates?

50:49.160 --> 50:51.980
Because there is a massive architectural catch.

50:52.560 --> 50:54.340
The paper highlights a crucial nuance.

50:55.160 --> 51:03.800
A Fredkin gate only realizes what are called weight-preserving maps, meaning the total number of ones and zeros going in must equal the number coming out.

51:03.800 --> 51:04.360
Okay.

51:04.740 --> 51:06.000
It can only shuffle things.

51:06.120 --> 51:08.060
It can't fundamentally change the balance.

51:08.680 --> 51:12.620
And Toffoli gates, without extra help, only realize even permutations.

51:12.700 --> 51:15.580
It can only swap things around in mathematically restricted ways.

51:15.900 --> 51:21.240
So a computer built entirely of just Toffoli gates couldn't actually calculate everything.

51:21.800 --> 51:24.480
It wouldn't be functionally complete like NAND.

51:24.820 --> 51:25.780
Not on its own, no.

51:26.040 --> 51:32.780
To compute everything reversibly to achieve universal computation, you must introduce ancillary bits.

51:33.000 --> 51:33.760
Ancillary bits.

51:33.880 --> 51:35.660
Extra blank memory space.

51:36.080 --> 51:45.820
You have to keep a physical record of the quote-unquote garbage calculations, the scratch pad work, so that you can run the whole process backward later to clean it up.

51:46.140 --> 51:51.560
This is Charles Bennett's famous compute, copy, and uncompute scheme, right?

51:51.580 --> 51:52.480
Yes, exactly.

51:52.840 --> 51:55.800
Walk me through how this actually works, because it sounds like magic.

51:56.120 --> 51:59.340
Imagine you are doing a complex math problem on a whiteboard.

51:59.340 --> 52:04.300
You write down all sorts of intermediate steps, carrying numbers, crossing things out.

52:05.060 --> 52:06.900
Eventually, you get the final answer.

52:07.460 --> 52:07.860
42.

52:07.860 --> 52:13.360
In a normal NAND computer, you just erase the whole whiteboard and keep the 42.

52:13.920 --> 52:17.200
But erasing the whiteboard costs thermodynamic heat.

52:17.420 --> 52:17.620
Right.

52:17.720 --> 52:18.260
The cloud bill.

52:18.600 --> 52:19.420
Landauer's principle.

52:19.960 --> 52:22.900
Bennett realized you don't have to erase it.

52:22.900 --> 52:28.600
Instead, you do the math forward, generating all that intermediate garbage on the whiteboard.

52:28.760 --> 52:28.980
Okay.

52:29.320 --> 52:33.560
You then copy the final answer, 42, onto a safe separate piece of paper.

52:34.060 --> 52:34.480
Got it.

52:34.480 --> 52:39.860
Then, you look at your whiteboard, and you run your perfectly reversible logic gates backward.

52:40.360 --> 52:41.580
You uncompute it.

52:41.920 --> 52:42.340
Yes.

52:42.560 --> 52:48.460
You systematically uncompute every single step, uncarrying the numbers, uncrossing things out,

52:48.600 --> 52:52.500
until your whiteboard is perfectly blank again, back in its initial state.

52:52.900 --> 52:56.020
You return the memory to zero without ever actually erasing it.

52:56.460 --> 52:57.820
You just untangle the knot.

52:58.300 --> 52:58.820
Exactly.

52:59.440 --> 53:03.420
And because you copied the final answer first, you still have the result of your computation.

53:03.420 --> 53:04.260
Wow.

53:04.820 --> 53:10.060
So the massive takeaway here is that functional completeness, the fact that we use irreversible

53:10.060 --> 53:15.120
NAND gates, does not establish a universal thermodynamic cost per operation.

53:15.480 --> 53:15.960
Exactly.

53:16.580 --> 53:17.800
Erasing data costs energy.

53:18.140 --> 53:24.560
But reversible uncomputation just costs memory space for the whiteboard and time to run it backward.

53:25.100 --> 53:25.460
Yes.

53:25.800 --> 53:31.800
Your laptop gets hot because we, as engineers, choose to use irreversible NAND gates that constantly

53:31.800 --> 53:33.900
dump data to save space and time.

53:34.200 --> 53:38.220
It is not because the fundamental mathematics of computation demands heat.

53:38.760 --> 53:39.280
Precisely.

53:39.600 --> 53:42.780
We trade thermodynamic efficiency for spatial efficiency.

53:43.140 --> 53:43.760
That's amazing.

53:43.760 --> 53:50.300
And this mathematical reversibility leads to a really fascinating side note about our Fibonacci

53:50.300 --> 53:50.840
sequence.

53:51.040 --> 53:51.340
Oh, yeah.

53:51.720 --> 53:56.700
If we build our Fibonacci register using reversible gates and it operates within a finite state

53:56.700 --> 54:01.700
space, meaning it has a limited amount of memory before it overflows, it is a mathematical

54:01.700 --> 54:05.080
certainty that the sequence must eventually loop back on itself.

54:05.600 --> 54:06.180
Oh, right.

54:06.180 --> 54:12.620
If you take Fibonacci numbers modulo something, like, say you only pay attention to the last

54:12.620 --> 54:15.560
digit of the number, they form a repeating pattern.

54:16.200 --> 54:19.300
The sequence of just the last digits loops forever.

54:19.680 --> 54:19.920
Yes.

54:20.460 --> 54:22.840
In mathematics, these are called Pisano periods.

54:23.540 --> 54:28.740
Any reversible mapping operating on a finite set of states must eventually return to its starting

54:28.740 --> 54:29.100
state.

54:29.500 --> 54:31.220
It's a topological guarantee.

54:31.220 --> 54:36.400
The paper notes this as a profound property of reversible physical systems.

54:37.280 --> 54:42.480
Speaking of how we store data in memory, the paper brings up a wild alternative to the

54:42.480 --> 54:44.280
standard binary zeros and ones.

54:45.020 --> 54:45.760
Yes, it does.

54:45.920 --> 54:48.680
It discusses balanced ternary encoding.

54:49.720 --> 54:51.400
This completely melted my brain.

54:51.980 --> 54:58.400
Instead of binary, which uses zero and one, balanced ternary uses three digits, minus one, zero,

54:58.400 --> 54:59.100
and one.

54:59.100 --> 55:03.180
Or, to make it visually simpler, minus zero and plus.

55:03.800 --> 55:04.040
Right.

55:04.940 --> 55:06.660
Why would anyone build a computer like this?

55:06.860 --> 55:10.780
Balanced ternary is actually mathematically beautiful, and it was used in some early Soviet

55:10.780 --> 55:12.300
computers, like the Seton.

55:12.660 --> 55:12.940
Really?

55:13.180 --> 55:13.420
Yeah.

55:13.820 --> 55:18.300
Because the numbering system intrinsically includes a negative digit, you do not need to

55:18.300 --> 55:22.740
waste a separate bit of memory just to indicate whether a number is positive or negative.

55:23.140 --> 55:25.520
The sign is built into the magnitude.

55:25.920 --> 55:26.800
Okay, that makes sense.

55:26.800 --> 55:31.840
The paper explains a brilliant parity rule for representing our Fibonacci numbers in balanced

55:31.840 --> 55:32.220
ternary.

55:32.500 --> 55:35.940
Okay, we are going to have to walk through this math carefully, because I want the listener

55:35.940 --> 55:38.080
to grasp how strange and cool this is.

55:38.400 --> 55:41.060
Give me an example of a number in balanced ternary.

55:41.440 --> 55:44.340
Let's take the eighth Fibonacci number, which is 21.

55:44.340 --> 55:50.940
In balanced ternary, the number 21 is encoded as a four-digit string, zero, plus, minus, plus.

55:51.600 --> 55:52.660
Okay, let's do the translation.

55:53.380 --> 55:56.760
In normal binary, the positions are worth one, two, four, eight, sixteen.

55:57.480 --> 56:02.620
In ternary, powers of three, the positions are worth one, three, nine, twenty-seven, eighty-one.

56:02.880 --> 56:03.260
Correct.

56:03.260 --> 56:09.680
So, if the string is zero, plus, minus, plus, that is zero twenty-sevenths, plus one, nine,

56:10.080 --> 56:12.240
minus one, three, plus one, one.

56:12.720 --> 56:17.400
So, zero plus nine is nine, minus three is six, plus one is seven.

56:17.520 --> 56:18.400
Wait, that's not 21.

56:18.640 --> 56:19.260
What did I do wrong?

56:19.640 --> 56:21.440
You read it left to right, like a human.

56:21.900 --> 56:22.140
Oh.

56:22.140 --> 56:26.460
In computer architecture, strings are often represented with the least significant digit first,

56:26.920 --> 56:30.080
meaning the rightmost digit of the math is on the left side of the string.

56:30.720 --> 56:34.100
Read zero plus, minus, plus from right to left in your math.

56:34.340 --> 56:34.600
Oh.

56:34.920 --> 56:35.200
Okay.

56:35.460 --> 56:36.260
Let's reverse it.

56:36.700 --> 56:43.060
The string zero plus, minus, plus means zero times one, the three to the zero position,

56:43.820 --> 56:46.840
plus one times three, the three to the one position,

56:47.860 --> 56:50.640
minus one times nine, the three squared position,

56:50.640 --> 56:54.060
plus one times twenty-seven, the three cubed position.

56:54.640 --> 56:55.460
Okay, add it up.

56:55.740 --> 56:56.300
Let's add that up.

56:56.940 --> 57:03.400
Zero plus three is three, minus nine is negative six, plus twenty-seven is twenty-one.

57:03.660 --> 57:04.100
Exactly.

57:04.540 --> 57:05.260
Okay, that makes sense.

57:05.680 --> 57:07.120
It's weird, but it works.

57:07.740 --> 57:10.280
But what is the parity rule the paper mentions?

57:11.340 --> 57:16.280
The paper proves a structural constraint on how Fibonacci numbers look when written this way.

57:16.280 --> 57:22.300
The rule is the total number of non-zero digits in the balanced ternary string is an even number

57:22.300 --> 57:25.820
exactly when the Fibonacci index is a multiple of three.

57:26.280 --> 57:26.760
Okay.

57:27.020 --> 57:28.780
Let's test that on our number twenty-one.

57:29.340 --> 57:31.580
Twenty-one is the eighth Fibonacci number.

57:32.540 --> 57:34.060
Eight is not a multiple of three.

57:34.320 --> 57:34.500
Right.

57:34.500 --> 57:40.320
So, the rule says the number of pluses and minuses in zero plus minus plus should be odd.

57:41.280 --> 57:45.960
And looking at it, it has three non-zero digits, two pluses and one minus.

57:46.580 --> 57:47.580
Three is odd.

57:47.640 --> 57:48.220
The rule holds.

57:48.600 --> 57:48.980
It does.

57:49.380 --> 57:51.480
That is such a bizarrely specific pattern.

57:51.660 --> 57:56.620
But it shows how deeply intertwined the structure of the math is with the physical representation of the data.

57:56.840 --> 57:57.300
It does.

57:57.300 --> 58:03.300
And the negation operation in balanced ternary is incredibly simple, which saves computation time.

58:03.740 --> 58:03.760
Also.

58:04.380 --> 58:09.940
To make the number twenty-one negative, to get negative twenty-one, you don't have to do two's complement math like in binary.

58:10.320 --> 58:14.300
You literally just flip every plus to a minus and every minus to a plus.

58:14.900 --> 58:19.560
So, zero plus minus plus becomes zero minus plus minus.

58:20.020 --> 58:20.420
Exactly.

58:20.420 --> 58:27.320
And that evaluates to zero minus three plus nine minus twenty-seven, which is negative twenty-one.

58:27.960 --> 58:30.360
No separate signed digit needed.

58:31.040 --> 58:31.780
That's so elegant.

58:32.460 --> 58:44.780
The broader point for the listener here is that the way we choose to encode information, whether we use unary tally marks, binary strings, or balanced ternary, fundamentally alters the physical footprint of the computation.

58:45.320 --> 58:45.560
Yes.

58:46.020 --> 58:47.580
Your data has physical weight.

58:47.580 --> 58:50.700
The representation changes the thermodynamic cost.

58:51.380 --> 58:54.100
Which perfectly sets up the final conceptual leap of the deep dive.

58:54.420 --> 58:59.020
We are moving to section six, the four layers of truth and accountable models.

58:59.320 --> 59:00.580
This is the big picture.

59:01.000 --> 59:01.240
Right.

59:01.540 --> 59:05.300
We have discussed mathematical specifications, like lambda calculus.

59:05.560 --> 59:08.980
We have discussed computer programs, like the rolling state.

59:09.460 --> 59:12.220
We have discussed physical circuits, like NAND gates.

59:12.440 --> 59:16.280
And we have discussed data representations, like balanced ternary.

59:16.280 --> 59:17.620
Four distinct layers.

59:18.020 --> 59:19.300
We have all these layers.

59:19.520 --> 59:23.260
But how do we know any of this is actually true in the real world?

59:23.700 --> 59:24.560
How do we prove it?

59:24.960 --> 59:27.020
This is the crux of the entire paper.

59:27.560 --> 59:29.660
Let me pose a hypothetical question to you.

59:29.860 --> 59:30.140
Okay.

59:30.140 --> 59:45.880
If a tech company shows you a video of a software benchmark for a brand new processor, and the video shows the processor running a complex calculation perfectly 25 times in a row without a single error, haven't they mathematically proven that their hardware and code works?

59:45.880 --> 59:47.760
I mean, my gut reaction is yes.

59:48.600 --> 59:52.800
If I watch a car drive around a track 25 times without crashing, I assume the car works.

59:53.620 --> 59:58.200
If the processor calculates the first 25 Fibonacci numbers perfectly, the logic must be sound.

59:58.620 --> 01:00:02.240
Your gut would be completely wrong in the realm of computer science.

01:00:02.340 --> 01:00:02.800
Okay, why?

01:00:02.800 --> 01:00:07.920
And this is why the author meticulously breaks down the four layers of correctness.

01:00:08.080 --> 01:00:14.580
As detailed in Appendix I and Section 15 of the source, we must separate validation from verification.

01:00:15.220 --> 01:00:16.620
Okay, let's break down the four layers.

01:00:16.740 --> 01:00:17.180
Layer 1.

01:00:17.660 --> 01:00:19.320
Layer 1 is the specification.

01:00:20.000 --> 01:00:22.480
This is the pure mathematical definition.

01:00:22.940 --> 01:00:30.520
For Fibonacci, it is the rule F0 equals 0, F1 equals 1, and Fn plus 2 equals Fn plus 1 plus Fn.

01:00:30.520 --> 01:00:32.340
How do we prove Layer 1?

01:00:33.040 --> 01:00:33.560
You don't.

01:00:33.860 --> 01:00:34.900
It needs no proof.

01:00:35.100 --> 01:00:36.000
It is an axiom.

01:00:36.180 --> 01:00:37.880
We simply all agree on the definition.

01:00:38.400 --> 01:00:40.200
If someone disagrees, they aren't wrong.

01:00:40.580 --> 01:00:42.420
They are just talking about a different sequence.

01:00:43.140 --> 01:00:43.480
Got it.

01:00:43.960 --> 01:00:44.540
Layer 2.

01:00:45.180 --> 01:00:46.320
Layer 2 is the program.

01:00:46.820 --> 01:00:50.680
This is the actual algorithm we use, like our rolling state sliding window.

01:00:51.140 --> 01:00:54.620
To prove this is mathematically correct, you cannot just test it 25 times.

01:00:54.800 --> 01:00:56.520
You must use mathematical induction.

01:00:56.900 --> 01:00:57.800
Mathematical induction.

01:00:57.940 --> 01:00:58.180
Yes.

01:00:58.180 --> 01:01:03.420
You must logically prove that if it works for state k, it absolutely must work for state

01:01:03.420 --> 01:01:07.220
k plus 1, and therefore it works for all infinity of n.

01:01:07.700 --> 01:01:11.040
You prove the logic holds forever without ever running the code.

01:01:11.620 --> 01:01:12.180
Layer 3.

01:01:12.420 --> 01:01:16.560
Layer 3 is the circuit, the physical 9 NAND gate adder.

01:01:16.780 --> 01:01:17.780
How do you prove this works?

01:01:18.180 --> 01:01:19.180
You don't use induction.

01:01:19.540 --> 01:01:21.220
You use finite local checks.

01:01:21.360 --> 01:01:21.940
Local checks.

01:01:21.940 --> 01:01:26.720
Because a 9 NAND gate adder only has a very small, finite number of possible electrical

01:01:26.720 --> 01:01:32.420
input combinations, specifically 8 combinations, for a full adder, you can simply exhaustively

01:01:32.420 --> 01:01:34.260
check every single physical path.

01:01:34.600 --> 01:01:36.640
Oh, just run all 8 possibilities.

01:01:37.020 --> 01:01:37.420
Exactly.

01:01:37.780 --> 01:01:41.520
If all 8 paths yield the correct output, the circuit is physically verified.

01:01:41.840 --> 01:01:43.140
And finally, layer 4.

01:01:43.620 --> 01:01:44.840
Layer 4 is representation.

01:01:45.260 --> 01:01:47.480
The binary or balanced ternary strings.

01:01:47.480 --> 01:01:51.120
You prove this layer is correct by ensuring a round-trip decoding.

01:01:51.520 --> 01:01:51.780
What's that?

01:01:52.100 --> 01:01:56.720
You mathematically prove that taking an abstract number, encoding it into ternary, and then

01:01:56.720 --> 01:02:01.380
immediately decoding it gives you the exact same abstract number back every single time

01:02:01.380 --> 01:02:02.560
with no data loss.

01:02:03.120 --> 01:02:06.920
So, going back to my video of the tech company's processor running 25 times.

01:02:07.220 --> 01:02:10.460
Testing the first 25 Fibonacci numbers is merely validation.

01:02:10.860 --> 01:02:12.520
It is empirical observation.

01:02:12.820 --> 01:02:14.420
It is not verification.

01:02:14.420 --> 01:02:15.800
Because it's not exhaustive.

01:02:15.960 --> 01:02:16.160
Right.

01:02:16.700 --> 01:02:22.500
A finite check of an unbounded, infinite domain proves absolutely nothing about the 26th number.

01:02:23.200 --> 01:02:27.260
The processor might have a physical flaw that only triggers on the 26th calculation.

01:02:27.580 --> 01:02:28.340
That makes a lot of sense.

01:02:29.120 --> 01:02:33.580
The layers of correctness require mathematical induction and exhaustive local checking, and

01:02:33.580 --> 01:02:36.480
not just watching a video and hoping the pattern continues.

01:02:36.840 --> 01:02:43.100
To visualize this difference between a static truth and a proven process, the paper uses a

01:02:43.100 --> 01:02:49.440
really brilliant contrast between Venn diagrams and directed acyclic graphs, or DAGs.

01:02:49.960 --> 01:02:50.400
Yes.

01:02:50.940 --> 01:02:53.020
We started this deep dive with a Venn diagram.

01:02:53.560 --> 01:02:58.120
Socrates is inside the circle of men, which is inside the circle of mortal things.

01:02:58.540 --> 01:03:01.340
A Venn diagram shows static class inclusion.

01:03:01.680 --> 01:03:01.920
Right.

01:03:02.180 --> 01:03:03.120
It's frozen in time.

01:03:03.120 --> 01:03:07.080
But a DAG, a directed acyclic graph, shows dependencies.

01:03:07.860 --> 01:03:10.700
It is a flowchart of time, causation, and computation.

01:03:11.440 --> 01:03:14.980
It is a series of nodes connected by arrows that only point forward.

01:03:15.320 --> 01:03:19.400
It shows which specific calculation relies on which previous calculation.

01:03:19.820 --> 01:03:20.040
Okay.

01:03:20.380 --> 01:03:21.920
So how does Fibonacci fit in?

01:03:22.360 --> 01:03:24.080
The Fibonacci sequence is a DAG.

01:03:24.620 --> 01:03:29.380
The calculation of the 10th number physically relies on the completion of the 8th and 9th.

01:03:29.420 --> 01:03:30.400
It flows forward.

01:03:30.400 --> 01:03:35.840
And this brings us to what might genuinely be my favorite part of the entire paper, the

01:03:35.840 --> 01:03:39.540
philosophical detour, the Disneyland analogy.

01:03:39.860 --> 01:03:40.060
Yes.

01:03:40.240 --> 01:03:43.860
Drawing on the work of the French postmodern philosopher Jean Bougiard.

01:03:44.220 --> 01:03:47.120
When I read this in the source, it blew my mind.

01:03:47.680 --> 01:03:51.560
The paper makes this incredibly deep point about simulation and reality.

01:03:51.980 --> 01:03:52.880
It's a great section.

01:03:54.020 --> 01:03:57.720
Disneyland announces itself as explicitly imaginary.

01:03:57.720 --> 01:03:59.880
It is a giant fake castle.

01:04:00.440 --> 01:04:01.880
There are giant mice walking around.

01:04:01.960 --> 01:04:04.980
It is explicitly, loudly, a fantasy world.

01:04:05.780 --> 01:04:06.020
Why?

01:04:06.820 --> 01:04:07.140
Why?

01:04:07.360 --> 01:04:08.260
According to Bougiard?

01:04:08.580 --> 01:04:08.840
Right.

01:04:09.700 --> 01:04:15.160
Bougiard argues that Disneyland presents itself as imaginary in order to make the outside world,

01:04:15.160 --> 01:04:19.920
the parking lot, Los Angeles, your normal life, feel real by contrast.

01:04:20.180 --> 01:04:20.580
Exactly.

01:04:20.580 --> 01:04:28.380
Even though the outside world is also just a constructed set of signs, scripts, artificial borders, and managed expectations.

01:04:28.800 --> 01:04:32.020
It is a profound analogy about how human beings process reality.

01:04:32.660 --> 01:04:36.020
How does this apply to mathematics and thermodynamics, though?

01:04:36.120 --> 01:04:36.300
Right.

01:04:36.780 --> 01:04:41.680
Think about a mathematical derivation, like our incredibly rigorous proof against perpetual motion,

01:04:41.880 --> 01:04:44.500
using Ville's inequality, and the super martingale.

01:04:45.120 --> 01:04:46.880
That math is a closed system.

01:04:47.220 --> 01:04:48.520
It announces its rules up front.

01:04:48.760 --> 01:04:49.400
Like Disneyland.

01:04:49.400 --> 01:04:50.220
Like Disneyland.

01:04:50.820 --> 01:04:53.580
It is perfectly true relative to its hypotheses.

01:04:53.900 --> 01:04:58.700
Remember H1 and H2, the super martingale hypothesis, and the boundary conditions?

01:04:59.000 --> 01:04:59.340
Right.

01:04:59.880 --> 01:05:05.660
If H1 and H2 are absolutely true, then the proof that perpetual motion is impossible is flawless.

01:05:06.060 --> 01:05:10.580
But does the physical, messy world actually obey H1 and H2?

01:05:11.000 --> 01:05:17.760
Do those abstract mathematical hypotheses flawlessly map onto the physical atoms in the heat engine sitting on the table?

01:05:17.760 --> 01:05:18.820
That's the real question.

01:05:18.820 --> 01:05:22.640
That requires what the paper calls real-world credence.

01:05:23.200 --> 01:05:24.560
The math is Disneyland.

01:05:25.060 --> 01:05:30.400
It is a perfect, pristine, imaginary enclosure that operates flawlessly on its own rules.

01:05:30.540 --> 01:05:30.700
Right.

01:05:30.920 --> 01:05:39.100
And because it is so perfect, it tricks us into thinking that the real world outside the physical engine operates with the same perfect mathematical certainty.

01:05:39.100 --> 01:05:41.880
But the real world is the parking lot.

01:05:41.880 --> 01:05:43.280
It is messy.

01:05:43.280 --> 01:05:50.160
The math proves that the probability of the conclusion, given the hypotheses, is mathematically 100%.

01:05:50.160 --> 01:05:59.040
But our confidence that those hypotheses actually truly apply to the physical engine we are looking at, that is entirely up to human empirical observation.

01:05:59.040 --> 01:05:59.520
Exactly.

01:05:59.520 --> 01:05:59.800
Exactly.

01:06:00.600 --> 01:06:03.960
The math cannot prove that the real world obeys the math.

01:06:04.160 --> 01:06:04.400
Wow.

01:06:04.780 --> 01:06:08.680
And this is why the author introduces the concept of laundering evidence.

01:06:09.100 --> 01:06:10.800
Returning to our DAG flow chart.

01:06:10.980 --> 01:06:11.260
Okay.

01:06:11.300 --> 01:06:12.580
What is laundering evidence?

01:06:12.580 --> 01:06:21.400
If you see a news story reposted a thousand times on social media, you might look at that massive volume of posts and think, wow, this must be incredibly true.

01:06:21.520 --> 01:06:22.340
Look at all this evidence.

01:06:22.480 --> 01:06:22.600
Right.

01:06:22.840 --> 01:06:23.240
Consensus.

01:06:23.240 --> 01:06:39.120
But if you trace the DAG back, if you follow the arrows of dependency backward, and you find that all 1,000 reposts trace back to a single unverified root node, a single anonymous account, you do not have 1,000 pieces of evidence.

01:06:39.460 --> 01:06:40.860
You have one piece of evidence.

01:06:41.280 --> 01:06:44.820
You just have a very loud, highly replicated single point of failure.

01:06:45.040 --> 01:06:45.420
Exactly.

01:06:45.420 --> 01:06:54.880
A perfectly rational person facing a supposed perpetual motion machine or facing a man claiming to be an immortal Socrates does exactly the same thing.

01:06:55.220 --> 01:06:56.080
They question the premises.

01:06:56.440 --> 01:06:56.660
Right.

01:06:56.860 --> 01:06:59.180
They don't try to break the laws of pure logic.

01:06:59.400 --> 01:07:01.860
They don't try to argue that 2 plus 2 equals 5.

01:07:02.080 --> 01:07:04.780
They trace the DAG back to the root node.

01:07:05.220 --> 01:07:11.240
They check if the pristine hypotheses of Disneyland actually apply to the messy reality of the parking lot.

01:07:11.420 --> 01:07:12.360
That is incredible.

01:07:12.360 --> 01:07:15.880
We have gone on a staggering journey today.

01:07:16.120 --> 01:07:16.760
We really have.

01:07:17.000 --> 01:07:30.520
From the ancient static syllogism of Socrates to the third-emode dynamic casinos and leaky buckets of Maxwell's demon to the nine-man gate architecture running the laptops in front of us, all the way to the philosophical parking lot of Disneyland.

01:07:31.060 --> 01:07:31.840
It's all connected.

01:07:31.840 --> 01:07:46.800
We've seen how the exact same intellectual tools, generalizing a state, taking a single microscopic step, and mathematically sliding the index forward in time, are used to solve fundamental mysteries across the entire spectrum of human knowledge.

01:07:46.800 --> 01:07:52.060
It truly exposes the underlying structural unity of how we model the world.

01:07:52.500 --> 01:08:02.720
Whether we are using pure logic, probabilistic physics, or discrete computation, we are always just trying to build a machine that reliably carries a state forward into the future.

01:08:02.720 --> 01:08:08.920
And so, to you, our listener, I want to leave you with a final provocative thought to mull over.

01:08:09.440 --> 01:08:10.200
A parting thought.

01:08:10.580 --> 01:08:19.200
We've established that our mathematical models are perfect closed systems, and physical reality is a messy place where we just have to hope our hypotheses apply.

01:08:19.700 --> 01:08:21.060
But think about your own mind.

01:08:21.060 --> 01:08:23.060
Hmm, this is a good one.

01:08:23.700 --> 01:08:33.460
When you learn something new, like everything we covered in this deep dive today, are you physically rewiring a messy, entropic, thermodynamic circuit in your biological brain?

01:08:33.980 --> 01:08:39.620
Or are you downloading a pure, logical truth into a closed mathematical system?

01:08:39.960 --> 01:08:41.280
The software versus the hardware.

01:08:41.620 --> 01:08:42.080
Exactly.

01:08:43.000 --> 01:08:48.620
If our proofs and our physics are entirely separate layers of reality, which layer is you?

01:08:48.620 --> 01:08:51.060
That is a question worth carrying forward.

01:08:52.060 --> 01:08:52.980
Stay curious, learner.

01:08:53.280 --> 01:08:54.120
Until next time.

